{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>I am YHWH.</b> Trustworthy to pay reward." "<b>Your Elohim.</b> Trustworthy to exact payment." "<b>Who brought you out.</b> On this condition I redeemed you — that you accept upon yourselves My decrees." "<b>I am YHWH your Elohim.</b> Why is it said again? So that Israel should not say: why did the Omnipresent say it? Is it not so that we act and receive reward? We do not act and do not receive reward — against your will I am your King. And thus it says (Ezekiel 20): ״If not with a strong hand … I will reign over you״ (Sifrei). Another interpretation: why is the exodus from Egypt mentioned? I am He who distinguished in Egypt between the drop of a firstborn and one that is not of a firstborn; I am He who is destined to distinguish and to exact payment from one who hangs [techelet dye] on his garment and says it is techelet. And from the foundation of Rabbi Moshe the Darshan I copied: why is the section of the wood-gatherer juxtaposed to the section of idolatry? To tell you that one who profanes the Sabbath is like one who worships idolatry, for it too is equivalent to all the commandments, and thus it says in Ezra (Nehemiah 9:14): ״And on Mount Sinai You came down and gave Your people Torah and commandments, and Your holy Sabbath You made known to them״; and also the section of tzitzit for this reason was juxtaposed to these, because it too is equivalent to all the commandments, as it says: ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֶת כָּל מִצְוֺתָי״." "<b>On the corners of their garments.</b> Corresponding to ״וָאֶשָּׂא אֶתְכֶם עַל כַּנְפֵי נְשָׁרִים״ (Exodus 19:4) — on four corners, and not one with three and not one with five, corresponding to four expressions of redemption that were said in Egypt: ״וְהוֹצֵאתִי וְהִצַּלְתִּי וְגָאַלְתִּי וְלָקַחְתִּי״ (Exodus 6)." "<b>A thread of tekhelet.</b> On account of the mourning of the firstborn — the Targum of שִׁכּוּל is תִּכְלָא — and their plague was at night, and so the color of tekhelet resembles the firmament darkening at eventide; and the eight threads in it correspond to the eight days that Israel waited from when they came out of Egypt until they said song at the Sea."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {3 "The phrase תולה קלא אילן refers to one who artificially dyes (hangs dye on) threads to pass them off as genuine techelet."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:11:16.685418632Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}